var _markdown = "\n\n## What is defineService? [#what-is-defineservice]\n\n`defineService` creates typed service objects for business logic that belongs neither in a controller nor in a model. A controller orchestrates the request; a model represents a row. Everything in between — billing runs, welcome emails, permission resolution reused across entry points — is a service. Services live in `src/app/services/`, one per file, authored with the same `defineX` grammar as everything else.\n\nThe point of a service is reuse without imports. Because services are declared and resolvable through the application container, any consumer can obtain one by name, and a controller, job, task, or event handler all get the same instance. See [Auto-Discovery](/docs/core-concepts/auto-discovery) for how the container picks services up.\n\n## Creating a Service [#creating-a-service]\n\n```ts title=\"src/app/services/email.ts\"\n// src/app/services/email.ts\nimport { defineService } from '@kwiva/services'\n\nexport default defineService('email', {\n  sendWelcome: async (user: User) => {\n    await mailer.send({\n      to: user.email,\n      subject: 'Welcome to our app',\n      template: 'welcome',\n      data: { name: user.name },\n    })\n  },\n\n  sendPasswordReset: async (user: User, token: string) => {\n    await mailer.send({\n      to: user.email,\n      subject: 'Reset your password',\n      template: 'password-reset',\n      data: { token },\n    })\n  },\n})\n```\n\nThe first argument names the service; the second is the service body — a plain object of methods. The service object is fully typed, so callers get method signatures and return types without annotation.\n\n## Using Services [#using-services]\n\nServices resolve through the application container:\n\n```ts title=\"using-services.ts\"\nimport { resolve } from '@kwiva/core'\nimport EmailService from '../services/email'\n\nconst email = resolve(EmailService)\nawait email.sendWelcome(user)\n```\n\nIn a handler, the same service is available without importing its definition by reaching through the container, or used directly from the controller module like any dependency:\n\n```ts title=\"using-services-2.ts\"\ndefineController('auth', (c) => ({\n  register: c.post('/register', async ({ body }) => {\n    const user = await User.create(body)\n    await EmailService.sendWelcome(user)\n    return user\n  }),\n}))\n```\n\nWhichever import style you prefer, the surface is the same typed methods — and because services resolve by name, swapping an implementation in tests replaces the behavior everywhere it is consumed.\n\n## Typed Injection [#typed-injection]\n\nService method signatures are typed against the application's models and other definitions, so a service can augment a typed model instance without constructing anonymous tuples:\n\n```ts title=\"src/app/services/payments.ts\"\n// src/app/services/payments.ts\nimport { defineService } from '@kwiva/services'\n\nexport default defineService('payments', {\n  invoiceFor: async (user: User, order: { amount: number }) => {\n    const vat = await config('billing.vatRate')\n    return { user: user.id, subtotal: order.amount, total: order.amount * (1 + vat) }\n  },\n})\n```\n\nTypes flow into service definitions the same way they flow everywhere else: model types come from `defineModel`, config values from the typed `config()` reader, and context values from `defineApp`. There is no separate type-declaration step for a service.\n\n## Service Composition [#service-composition]\n\nServices compose like any plain module — one service calls another, and both sit in the same container:\n\n```ts title=\"src/app/services/onboarding.ts\"\n// src/app/services/onboarding.ts\nimport { defineService } from '@kwiva/services'\n\nexport default defineService('onboarding', {\n  runFor: async (user: User, { resolve }) => {\n    await resolve('email').sendWelcome(user)\n    await resolve('audit').record('user.joined', user.id)\n  },\n})\n```\n\nKeep services discrete: one responsibility, one file, explicit dependencies. A service that wires other services together is still a service with a single job.\n\n## Service Lifecycle and Scoping [#service-lifecycle-and-scoping]\n\nServices are plain objects resolved from the container, with a clear ownership story:\n\n* **One instance per service** — a service resolves to a single instance for the application lifetime. State you keep on the service object is app-scoped, shared across every request.\n* **Per-request data comes from the context, never module state** — if a service needs the current user, tenant, or request ID, the caller passes the relevant context values, or the service reads them through a `resolve` value from `defineApp`. This keeps services request-agnostic and trivially testable.\n* **No lazy surprises** — a service is created when first resolved; dependencies it declares are resolved at call time, so circular imports between services are avoided by keeping them each a single unit.\n\n## Naming and Placement [#naming-and-placement]\n\nServices follow the naming conventions of the [defineX grammar](/docs/core-concepts/definex): one service per file in `src/app/services/`, kebab-case filenames, and a name matching the file. `src/app/services/billing.ts` defines the `billing` service, resolvable by that name everywhere the container is reachable.\n\nThe generators scaffold the shape: `kwiva make:service billing` writes `src/app/services/billing.ts` as a typed, discoverable starting point. See [Generators](/docs/cli/generators).\n\n## Where Services Are Consumed [#where-services-are-consumed]\n\nThe container is reachable from every entry point the framework provides, so one service implementation serves all of them:\n\n| Entry point     | Consumption                                           |\n| --------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |\n| Controllers     | in handlers, resolved or imported like any dependency |\n| Jobs, tasks     | within the handler context                            |\n| Event listeners | when an event is consumed                             |\n| Commands        | inside the command's `handle`                         |\n| Other services  | through explicit `resolve` of the dependency          |\n| Tests           | direct instantiation or container resolution          |\n\nBecause consumption is uniform, moving logic from a controller into a service does not change how it is called — it changes where the logic lives.\n\n## When to Reach for a Service [#when-to-reach-for-a-service]\n\nUse a service when the logic satisfies at least one of:\n\n* it is reused by more than one entry point (controller, job, task, event handler, command);\n* it coordinates multiple models, so putting it in any single model would be wrong;\n* it is a side-effecting integration (email, billing, storage uploads) that should be swappable in tests;\n* it is domain logic that a handler should not read.\n\nKeep plain query logic in the model, and keep request orchestration in the controller. The service layer is the middle: domain operations that neither belong to one row nor to one request.\n\n## Testing Services [#testing-services]\n\nBecause services are plain objects resolved from a container, unit tests instantiate them directly or through the container without the HTTP pipeline:\n\n* Direct: import the service definition and call its methods.\n* Container-based: resolve the service like the app does, and swap dependent services for fakes.\n* Integration: with `withApp`, a service resolves against the real kernel — useful when a service touches models or providers.\n\nSee [Testing](/docs/testing) for the harnesses that support each style.\n\n## What's Next [#whats-next]\n\n1. [Applications](/docs/core-concepts/applications) — how `defineApp` bootstraps the container services resolve from\n2. [Context](/docs/core-concepts/context) — the request object services are consumed from\n3. [Controllers](/docs/http/controllers) — where most services are called from\n4. [API: services](/api/services) — the `@kwiva/services` reference\n5. [Modules](/docs/core-concepts/modules) — how a module contributes services to the app\n";
